RD EXAMPLE_GENERAL R:RULE-M-0027 P:RULE-M-0479 | P: meaning of 403 (skill detail).
RD OVERLAP R:RULE-M-0027 P:RULE-M-0480 | R: 401/403 and deliberate 404 hiding; P: 404 when the resource does not exist or is deliberately hidden, plus the empty-collection rule.
SPLIT X:BF-M-0082 | a@121-150: Use 406 when no acceptable response representation can be produced and 415 when the request media type is unsupported; do not confuse them. | b@154-160: Use 422 for structurally valid requests with invalid semantic/business content; follow an established 400-for-both convention, otherwise 400 for contract errors and 422 for semantic errors. | c@169-178: Use 428 when a contract-required precondition such as If-Match is absent; other mandatory preconditions only when the contract defines them. | d@182-184: A rate-limited request returns 429 with Retry-After when the retry time is known; validation failures are not represented as rate limiting.
SD P:EXTRA-M-0157 X:BF-M-0082#a | S: HTTP content negotiation responses M: P MUST, X no modality E: none in either T: none | X part a anchor spans the 406 and 415 paragraphs; the 409/412 text between them is not asserted
SD P:EXTRA-M-0158 X:BF-M-0082#b | S: HTTP validation responses M: P DEFAULT, X no modality E: established 400-for-both convention (both) T: none
SD P:EXTRA-M-0159 X:BF-M-0082#c | S: HTTP preconditions M: P MUST, X no modality E: other preconditions only when the contract defines them (both) T: none
RD COMPOUND_PART P:EXTRA-M-0153 X:BF-M-0082#d | P adds 'inbound rate limiting is distinct from outbound resilience' (resilience reference).
RD EXAMPLE_GENERAL R:RULE-M-0028 P:RULE-M-0481 | R: 409 for state conflicts; P lists the 409 cases and excludes malformed syntax.
RD BROADER_NARROWER R:RULE-M-0028 P:RULE-M-0482 | R/rule covers 409 and 412; P (skill): a stale If-Match is normally 412, not 409.
RD OVERLAP R:RULE-M-0073#a P:RULE-M-0483 | R: unexpected failures go to centralized handling; P: 500 for unexpected failures via centralized handling and ProblemDetails without internals.
SPLIT X:BF-M-0083 | a@196-213: 502 and 504 are only for an API acting as an intermediary (invalid upstream response, upstream timeout); do not map every dependency failure or TaskCanceledException to them. | b@202-205: 503 for temporary inability, with Retry-After when a meaningful retry time is known. | c@221-231: With an idempotency key, reuse of the same key with a materially different payload is a reasonable 409, and idempotency is not claimed merely because duplicates are unlikely. | d@235-236: When HTTP optimistic concurrency is part of the contract prefer standard conditional request semantics over custom status codes.
SD P:EXTRA-M-0160 X:BF-M-0083#a | S: 502/504 responses M: P MUST_NOT, X no modality E: none in either T: none
SD P:EXTRA-M-0161 X:BF-M-0083#b | S: 503 responses M: P DEFAULT, X no modality E: none in either T: none
RD COMPOUND_PART X:BF-M-0083#c P:RULE-M-0485 | X part c also covers the 409 for reused keys with different payloads; P: do not claim idempotency merely because duplicates are unlikely.
SD P:RULE-M-0486 X:BF-M-0083#d | S: HTTP optimistic concurrency M: P PREFER, X no modality E: none in either T: none
RD OVERLAP R:RULE-M-0079 P:RULE-M-0484 | R: client cancellation is not a server failure; P: distinguish client-cancelled requests from timeouts when choosing a response.
RD EXAMPLE_GENERAL R:RULE-M-0024 P:RULE-M-0487 | R: HTTP semantics belong to the API layer; P: Controllers translate Application outcomes into HTTP outcomes.
# r387-r401 httpclient-factory SKILL.md
RD OVERLAP R:RULE-M-0062 P:RULE-M-0488 | R: Infrastructure translates remote responses; P: choose the simplest client model, outbound clients normally live in Infrastructure.
